---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Anatomy of a fan-out — Fathom">
  <article class="article-wrap">
    <nav class="breadcrumb">
      <a href="/blog" data-i18n="postAnatomy.blog">Blog</a>
      <span class="sep">›</span>
      <span data-i18n="postAnatomy.crumb1">Architecture</span>
      <span class="sep">›</span>
      <span data-i18n="postAnatomy.crumb2">Fan-out</span>
    </nav>

    <div class="article-date" data-i18n="postAnatomy.date">August 10, 2026 · 7 min read</div>
    <span class="article-tag" data-i18n="postAnatomy.tag">Architecture</span>
    <h1 class="article-title" data-i18n="postAnatomy.title">Anatomy of a fan-out: one question, a fleet of agents</h1>

    <p class="article-intro" data-i18n="postAnatomy.intro">Give Fathom a mission and the coordinator decomposes it, fans it out to a fleet of workers, collects their outputs under token budgets, lets an LLM judge compare the result against the goal, and synthesizes a deliverable. Research is one mission; the same pipeline supports code, operations, and knowledge work. Here is the whole pipeline — and the failure modes it is built to survive.</p>

    <div class="highlights">
      <h4 data-i18n="postAnatomy.highlightsTitle">Highlights</h4>
      <ul>
        <li data-i18n="postAnatomy.hl1">Plan → Fan-out → Collect → Reflection → Synthesize, driven by one coordinator</li>
        <li data-i18n="postAnatomy.hl2">Sub-agents run as tokio tasks in a JoinSet or as isolated OS processes</li>
        <li data-i18n="postAnatomy.hl3">Results come back as budget-capped summaries, never raw transcripts</li>
        <li data-i18n-html="postAnatomy.hl4">Goal Mode: an LLM judge runs up to <code>replan_rounds</code> gap-filling rounds</li>
        <li data-i18n="postAnatomy.hl5">Doom-loop detector and cancellation tokens guard the whole agent tree</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="article-body">
      <h2 data-i18n="postAnatomy.h2_1">Plan first, then fan out</h2>
      <p data-i18n-html="postAnatomy.p1">Every run starts with the <code>Coordinator</code>. The LLM decomposes the query into sub-tasks, and those sub-tasks are persisted to the database before anything spawns — so a run can be inspected, resumed, and audited. Only then does the fan-out begin: each sub-task gets its own <code>AgentRuntime</code>, either as a tokio task inside a <code>JoinSet</code> (the default — one process, lightweight) or, with <code>use_multiprocess = true</code>, as a separate OS process managed by the <code>ProcessManager</code> and talking over Unix domain sockets.</p>
      <p data-i18n-html="postAnatomy.p1b">The fleet is not homogeneous on purpose. Workers receive tools suited to the mission — web and document tools for research, file and code tools for engineering, or operator-defined profiles for other workflows. Personas and TOML profiles pre-wire prompts, models, and depth limits. Each depth-0 worker also receives a memory digest in its prompt, so the fleet starts with what previous runs already learned.</p>

      <h2 data-i18n="postAnatomy.h2_2">Collect with budgets, not transcripts</h2>
      <p data-i18n="postAnatomy.p2">A fan-out is only as useful as its collection and review steps. Sub-agents do not hand their full context back up — their results pass through a budget capper that trims them to summaries. That keeps the coordinator's own context small enough to reason over the whole fleet, while raw findings stay persisted in SQLite for verification. Ordering and attribution survive the trip: every summary is traceable to the sub-task and agent that produced it.</p>

      <h2 data-i18n="postAnatomy.h2_3">Reflection and the LLM judge</h2>
      <p data-i18n-html="postAnatomy.p3">Collection is followed by reflection. For missions with explicit completion criteria, the coordinator can run bounded gap rounds when required outputs are missing. Then, if Goal Mode is on, an LLM judge compares the collected result against the <em>original goal</em> — not against the plan. Where it names concrete gaps, new gap-filling sub-tasks are spawned; the loop runs for up to <code>replan_rounds</code> rounds. The judge is what stops a run from "completing the plan" while missing the point.</p>

      <h2 data-i18n="postAnatomy.h2_4">Synthesize, export, notify</h2>
      <p data-i18n-html="postAnatomy.p4">Finally the coordinator synthesizes: the LLM merges findings into a single report, written as <code>index.md</code>, <code>summary.md</code> and a <code>findings/</code> directory. The session summary and discovered contacts are absorbed into long-term memory — the next fan-out starts smarter. Then optional export (PDF, HTML, JSON, DOCX), notification (webhook, email, Telegram), or integration sync can be operator-triggered. One mission in; an inspectable deliverable out.</p>

      <h2 data-i18n="postAnatomy.h2_5">Failure is part of the design</h2>
      <p data-i18n-html="postAnatomy.p5">Fleets fail in ways single agents do not, so the control plane assumes it. The <code>DoomLoopDetector</code> watches for three identical tool calls in a row and stops the loop before it burns the budget. Cancellation tokens propagate through the entire agent tree, so cancelling a run cancels every child; a shell failure cascades to sibling tool calls instead of leaving half-executed batches. In multiprocess mode each worker is an isolated OS process with <code>kill_on_drop</code> semantics — one crash never takes down the fleet — and the coordinator watches stall timeouts: warn at 450 seconds, kill at 1200.</p>

      <blockquote data-i18n-html="postAnatomy.quote">
        The coordinator's job is not to run agents. It is to keep a budget, hold the goal, and know when to stop — everything else is mechanics.
        <cite>— Fathom runtime architecture notes</cite>
      </blockquote>

      <h2 data-i18n="postAnatomy.h2_6">The same loop, one level down</h2>
      <p data-i18n-html="postAnatomy.p6">Each sub-agent is itself the standard agent loop: build the prompt in three cache tiers, check the doom loop, estimate tokens and compact if needed, stream the LLM, pass tool calls through deny/approval/hook gates, execute the batch (reads in parallel, writes serialized), and loop until done. The fan-out is just that loop, multiplied — with a coordinator on top that keeps the multipliers honest.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="related-posts">
      <h3 data-i18n="postAnatomy.related">Related Posts</h3>
      <div class="related-grid">
        <a class="related-card" href="/blog/benchmarks-repo-map" data-i18n="postAnatomy.related1">The benchmark that caught a 31× slowdown</a>
        <a class="related-card" href="/blog/memory-that-remembers" data-i18n="postAnatomy.related2">A memory that remembers: append-only knowledge for autonomous workers</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .article-wrap { max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 80px 24px; }

  .breadcrumb { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 13px; color: var(--grey-600); margin-bottom: 32px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .breadcrumb a { color: var(--grey-600); transition: color .15s; }
  .breadcrumb a:hover { color: var(--grey-900); }
  .breadcrumb .sep { color: var(--grey-400); }

  .article-date { font-size: 15px; color: var(--grey-600); margin-bottom: 14px; }
  .article-tag { display: inline-block; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 11px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .6px; color: var(--grey-600); background: var(--grey-100); border: 1px solid var(--grey-200); padding: 4px 12px; border-radius: 100px; margin-bottom: 20px; }
  .article-title { font-size: clamp(28px, 4vw, 44px); line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: -.025em; font-weight: 500; }
  .article-intro { margin-top: 28px; font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--grey-700); }

  .highlights { margin: 36px 0; background: var(--grey-50); border: 1px solid var(--grey-200); border-radius: 10px; padding: 24px 28px; }
  .highlights h4 { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 12px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .6px; color: var(--grey-600); margin-bottom: 14px; }
  .highlights ul { list-style: none; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px; }
  .highlights li { position: relative; padding-left: 18px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--grey-800); }
  .highlights li::before { content: ""; position: absolute; left: 0; top: 8px; width: 6px; height: 6px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--grey-900); }
  .highlights code { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: .85em; background: var(--bg-card); border: 1px solid var(--grey-200); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 5px; }

  .article-body h2 { font-size: clamp(20px, 2.8vw, 28px); margin: 48px 0 16px; letter-spacing: -.02em; line-height: 1.15; }
  .article-body p { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--grey-800); margin-bottom: 18px; }
  .article-body code { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: .85em; background: var(--grey-100); border: 1px solid var(--grey-200); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 5px; }

  .article-body blockquote { margin: 40px 0; padding: 28px 32px; border-left: 3px solid #181818; background: var(--bg-card); border-radius: 0 10px 10px 0; font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--grey-800); font-style: italic; }
  .article-body blockquote cite { display: block; margin-top: 18px; font-style: normal; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 13px; color: var(--grey-600); }

  .related-posts { margin-top: 56px; padding-top: 32px; border-top: 1px solid var(--grey-200); }
  .related-posts h3 { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 12px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .6px; color: var(--grey-600); margin-bottom: 16px; }
  .related-grid { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px; }
  .related-card { display: block; border: 1px solid var(--grey-200); border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 20px; background: var(--bg-card); font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--grey-800); transition: border-color .15s, color .15s; }
  .related-card:hover { border-color: var(--grey-500); color: var(--grey-900); }
</style>
